Date: Tue, 2 Nov 93 15:44:15 PST
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Future Science

New thread: how will science be done in the future?  I submit that
the next 20 years will drastically alter the face of science.  The
engines for this revolution are brewing in computer science labs today.  
Furthermore, the coming revolution will increase the speed of many kinds 
of science by orders of magnitude and revolutionize our technological
base in the process.  Most of the skills of most of today's scientists
will be rendered obsolete, at a vastly greater speed than blue
collar workers, for example, will be replaced by robots.

There are many ways computer science is bringing on the automation
of science -- automated data collection, typesetting software,
statistical analysis software, advanced math software, cellular
automata-based supercomputer simulations. etc.  One of the main
engines of the coming revolution is less than five years old and has 
yet to make its mark in the scientific world: genetic programming (GP).   
GP performs Darwinian evolution; it can breed anything Turing-equivalent --
nonlinear math models,  mathematical proofs, algorithms, scientific 
theories, engineering designs, you name it -- as long as (a) the model can 
possibly be constructed from a language, and (b) some models are 
objectively better than others -- they explain the data with smaller 
error, they are more parsiminious, etc.   Here other aspects of computer
science can come into play, for example the use of Kolmogorov complexity
to provide an objective measure of parsimony.   As long as (a) and
(b) are met, GP can bring vast search spaces, with topologies
beyond the comprehension of humans and sizes beyond the capabilities 
of the most commonly used computer algorithms, to their knees.

In other words, GP takes the Darwinian algorithm from its previous range 
of applicability, the primordial 20-letter amino acid alphabet, to the 
range of practically every form of representation important to human 
civilization.  

GP can easily evolve designs and theories that no human understands.
So easily, that the most difficult problem is simplifying and
pretty-printing the resulting complex web of logic, math, etc.
for consumption of the human scientist.  Of course, this problem
already exists for many kinds of knowledge today.  No one person knows the 
entire E. coli genetic code.  "We know" the sequence, but the only 
thing that has fully memorized it is a computer database.  No one 
person will ever understand even a major fraction of the human brain 
or a 1990s era VLSI chip. etc., etc.  GP merely takes this trend another 
step.

GP could be looked on as merely accelerating the trend towards
the automation of science, which has been going on for
some time now.  Or it could looked on as revolutionizing science
far beyond the bounds of current capability or recognition --
the Science Singularity.

Here are application areas of GP which can specifically be foreseen
today:

* algorithm design:
	-- pseudorandom sequence generators (Koza has used GP to
		design algorithm with highest entropy)
	-- compression algorithms (a Bay Area company is currently
		looking at this)
	-- cryptography (eg, better trapdoor one-way functions)
	-- searching, sorting, etc.
	-- machine control
	-- meta-GP: using GP to find good GP strategies
	  (parameters, selection functions, ADF strategies, etc.) for 
		large classes of problems: GP, evolve thyself!
	-- combined data structure/algorithm design
	-- optimization of science simulations for certain regimes
	(eg quick-running approximations of Schrodinger's equation
	for diamondoid materials).
* computer-aided design:
	-- silicon compilers/circuit layout (augment & generalize the
		capabilities of current simulated-annealing based engines)
	-- compiler optimization
	-- mechanical design optimization
	-- design of new processes (areas where phenomenon are understood
	   well enough to be simulated, but how best to take advantage 
		of them is not -- nanotechnology has been mentioned)
* automated discovery of predictive models
	-- physics & chemistory (esp. areas where results can be 
		expressed mathematically, eg stellar physics)
	-- econometric models & financial forecasting
		(eg, track wagering GP project)
	-- experimental mathematics & theoretical computer science
		(eg, use GP instead of exhaustive search to statistically
		characterize soon-halting TMs, a al Chaitin's Omega)
	-- GP hooked up to high-frequency labs 
		(eg, 1,000 experiments per second for
		characterization & design of new molecules,
		via quantum control laser apparatus).

We have many gnawing mysteries in modern science, many of them
baffling because of the subtle, complex models involved:
the new gamma ray bursters discovered by GRO, the solar
neutrino deficit, the source of heat in some cold "fusion"
experiments, quantum dot phenomenon, the behavior of
superconductors with imperfections, etc.  The
rapid, automated evolution of complex nonlinear models
will cut through many of these problems like a hot knife 
through butter.  In the process, theorized phenomenon like
wormholes, magnetic monopoles, etc. etc. may either be
found or strongly falsified, as a new generation of GP-enabled
automated labs takes to work out the vast complex of possible
theories relating to these phenomenon, and in many cases to
test them out with the automated laboratories of the 21st 
century.

