The significant paper is A.N. Kolmogorov, "Logical basis for
information theory and probability theory", _IEEE Trans. on Inform.
Theory_ 14 (5) (1968) 662-664. There is an interesting earlier paper,
"Three approaches to the quantitative definition of information"
_Problems Inform. Transmission_ 1 (1) 1965 1-7, in which Kolmogorov
compares combinatorial, probabalistic and computational approaches.  I
think it is in that paper that he speculates on the amount of
information in a cockroach. Chaitin was onto some of the same ideas
just a bit later.

For a good recent review of the field of complexity theory and its
relation to information, with most of the significant results, if not
all, see Ming Li and Paul Vitanyi, "Kolmogorov complexity and its
applications", in J. van Leeuwen (ed) _Handbook of Theoretical
Computer Sceince_ (Elsevier, 1990). It is mathematically precise. An 
earlier shorter (compressed?) version can be found in _Proc. 3rd
IEEE Structure in Complexity Theory Conference_ (1988) 80-102. The
references in the expanded version are very complete.

I am not very keen on the uncertainty approach to information. It
suggests an uncertainty approach to entropy as well (like that adopted
by Jaynes). This view is regarded with considerable suspicion among
the best philosophers of science working in the area (see articles by
Sklar and Earman in PSA 1986, Vol 2 209-236, Philosophy of Science
Association, 1987). Furthermore, the equivalence of the complexity vs.
probabalistic approaches allows information to be defined in
strucutural terms (as a function of complexity), although it should be
recognised that the complexity of a structure will be relative to what
are the fundamental components of interest.  Popperian propensities
are justified by this approach, and made precise. The simplest case of
fundamental components are binary distinctions, the common currency of
information theory. Uncertainty is a relation between two things, at
least, and can be defined in terms of relative complexities, so it
really involves mutual information, rather than the information in a
string. The former is defined in terms of the latter in Kolmogorov 
theory. Perhaps the opposite can be done as well. If so, my choice
is merely a preference, not objective. If not, there is something
incomplete about the uncertainty choice, so it is defective.

On another issue that came up, mechanism and determinism, there are
some well-worked and precise philosophical studies. One version of
mechanism (used by David Bohm in _Causality and Chance in Modern
Physics_) has it that a system is mechanistic iff it can be described
completely with finite recursive means (explicitly definable, to use
Russell's terminology; Russell also suggested that deterministic was
equivalent to explicitly definable).  Richard Montague, _Formal
Philosophy_ (Yale, 1974) 303-359, gives a formal definition of a
deterministic theory. A theory is deterministic iff for two histories
satisfying the theory, if states of both at some time t0 are
identical, then their states at any other time are identical, roughly,
very roughly. It turns out that classical particle mechanics is not
deterministic, but that it is if we have specified the force function.
Let a system be a model of a theory, so a deterministic system is a
model of a deterministic theory. One unusual result is that there are
deterministic systems that are not periodic _and_ vice versa!

Using Russellian determinism (Bohm's mechanism), Montague proves that
there are exceptions to Russell's conjecture in both directions. This
justifies the claim that not all deterministic systems are
predictable, since to be predictable, a system must be explicitly
definable. But a system must also be deterministic to be predictable,
so their are unpredictable mechanical systems as well. This may be
quite surprising to most people. We tend to think of Classical
Mechanics as predictable, but it is neither deterministic (unless we
specify a force function -- but even this allows problematic cases --
see Keith Hutchison, _British Journal for the Philosophy of Science_
44 (1993) 305-322), nor predictable even if it is deterministic. For
example, consider a two planet system under gravity and dissipative
forces. Mercury and the Sun, for example, form such a system to a
reasonable approximation. There are a number of attractors with
varying probabilities of attraction. In fact, Mercury ended up in a
3/2 rotation/revolution ratio, which has about 30% likelihood given no
information about initial conditions. The boundary between, say, the
3/2 and 1/1 attractors will be a fractal, with no explicity
specifiable boundary (where does a particle on the boundary go?).
as Earman points out in _A Primer on Determinism_ (Reidel, 198?),
there can be regions of measure 0 that are indeterministic even in
classical mechanics with specicified forces. Classical Mecahnics
is clearly mechanical, and fits Bohm's definition. It fits neither
the definitions of predictable nor deterministic. Strange, but true.

I hope these points from logic and physical theory help to clarify
the discussion. You can fiddle around with the definitions as much
as you want, but I doubt that you will get a set that works better
than these in terms of our intuitions and customary usage in 
situations where they can be disitnguished (e.g., making predictions
obviously requires explicit definition + determinism, classical
mechanics is obviously mechanical, and Montague's definition
of determinism is inutitively very appealing).

